Hi Josh,

My name is Jennifer Taylor and I am the Digital Marketing Specialist here at
PostcardMania.

 

My job is to look at your website and offer suggestions for improvement to
help with your Everywhere Small Business campaign. We can certainly get you
the clicks with Everywhere Small Business, but it is your responsibility to
convert the leads.

 

With that being said, I looked over https://www.happyroof.com/  and here's
what I recommend.

 

Add your promotion and/or call to action to the top of the homepage, where
it's visible without having to scroll.

10% OFF Any Roofing Job 

Mention code POSTCARD26

FREE estimate

FREE roof inspection

Up to $1,000 for Anyone who refers us a roofing job that we take on.

 

 You could use a different code for online leads.

Use a bright, non-matching, contrasting color above the fold where it cannot
be missed. You could do this on a promotional bar, like this example.
<https://pcmbuilder.com/dental-sample-site-3pg/> 

 

Your campaign is built up of more than just postcards. The online ads are
sending traffic to your website. 

 

Your ads will likely mention your promotion-unless you've told us otherwise.
Naturally, prospects will expect to see the promo on the website after
clicking the ad. They'll expect to have to fill out a contact form in
exchange for the offer, or be told to call to redeem. 

 

Clearly state what you want them to do on your website. I recommend asking
for the bare minimum: an email, in exchange for the promo.

 

If you feel that you need more to initiate that first touch, please hide the
asterisks so people don't feel obligated, even if they are obligated, to
share that information at that time. Instead of "Submit," use "Send." As it
reads friendlier.

 

CAUTION: Excluding the promotion from your site could cause people to leave
your site without taking action. Most of the time, they're clicking the ad
to get the offer, so to exclude it from your site would be a disruption of
the sales cycle, and could lead to fewer online conversions. 

 

Here's the prospect's expected flow: 

*	Prospect sees your ad 
*	They click your ad 
*	Land on your website 
*	Fills out a form to redeem the offer (Converted Lead!)
*	You contact them ASAP for next steps (or they use a promo code to
purchase online, etc.)

 

If you decide to exclude the offer from your site, you are only using a
fraction of your marketing campaign to convert leads. 




Here's what that would look like for the online campaign results:

*	Prospect gets postcard in the mail
*	If they're interested, 95% of people will look you up online before
picking up the phone. Browse and leave.
*	Prospect sees an ad from the same company they received a postcard
from recently with the limited time special
*	Clicks ad 
*	Lands on your website again
*	Prospect leaves the website (confused/frustrated) after a few
seconds, because they don't see the promo that brought them there.
*	Sees ad again, might click ad/might not, because they already know
the call to action is missing.

 

If you need help, we sell standalone pop-up forms
<https://www.postcardmania.com/products-services/catch-that-lead-tool/>  and
complete pop-up form
<https://www.postcardmania.com/products-services/catch-and-close/> & email
drip funnels. These services are not included with Everywhere Small
Business, please let me know if you'd like more info.

 

I suggest you type out your number on the click-to-call buttons so people
viewing the site on a device other than a cell phone (EX: computer) can
still see your number and call you.



 

Your homepage has a button that says, "Meet the Team," but your team isn't
on the About page it leads to.

 

Add staff photos and bios to your About page. This personal touch will help
raise trust between the prospect and your company, making them more
comfortable initiating a first contact.

 

Questions to ask yourself when writing a personal bio -

Inspiration for starting the business. What problems were you looking to
solve? How long have you lived in the area? Do you have a family? Hobbies?
Charities you support? Organizations you're involved with? Passions? Pets?
Won any awards? Have any special certifications? The more personal the
better. Including personal info makes it easier for people to connect with
you and remember you. It also makes it easier for them to get comfortable
with you. You want them to want to reach out to you so make yourself known!

 

Please let me know if you have questions. 

 

If you do not know how to make the changes yourself or simply do not have
the time, we can create a new website and/or a pop-up form for you!

*We're not able to make changes to third-party websites that we did not
create, however, you are qualified for our
<https://www.postcardmania.com/products-services/catch-that-lead-tool/>
pop-up forms! 

 

I'd like to see the site again once you've made the edits. 

 

Thank you,

 

 

 


  <https://tools.mypostcardmania.com/reps/jennifertaylors.jpg> 

Jennifer Taylor

Digital Marketing Specialist

800-628-1804 x601

 <https://www.postcardmania.com/> PostcardMania.com

  <https://cdn.postcardmania.com/pcm27y.png> 


  <https://cdn.postcardmania.com/pcmsig2021q1.png> 

	


P.S. 2026 is here! Ask me about discounts for pre-scheduling your marketing
for the year.

	

 


